    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d118">

                <pb facs="0150" n="80" sameAs="frus:frus1934v04/pg80"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.3415/4152: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gilbert</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-09-21T13:00:00-04:00">September 21, 1934—1
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>96. Your 251, September 20, 11 a.m., last paragraph.<anchor n="55"
                        xml:id="d118-f1"/> You are authorized to make, orally and informally, the
                    clarifying statement to Avenol which you suggest based upon the contents of
                    Department’s 87, August 30, 4 p.m. and the statement further contained in the
                    first sentence of the Department’s 91, September 18, noon.<anchor n="56"
                        xml:id="d118-f2"/></p>
                <p>You should, of course, make it perfectly clear that the position assumed by this
                    Government is due solely to its belief that as a practical question the existing
                    negotiations offer a favorable opportunity for success and that it is in no
                    sense due to any desire to minimize the importance or the helpful character of
                    the League’s negotiations. If the negotiations now being conducted by Argentina,
                    Brazil, and the United States, in their present form or enlarged in scope by the
                    additional participation of other American Republics, prove to be fruitless, the
                    League will, of course, later be warranted in resuming actively its previous
                    negotiations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d118-f1" n="55" xml:id="d118-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d118-f2" n="56" xml:id="d118-f2-content">
                <p>The sentence reads, “This Government is unwilling to accept membership on any
                    Committee appointed by the League in connection with the Chaco dispute.”
                    Telegram No. 91 not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
